<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jabal al-Druze</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jebel Druze, Jabal al-Arab, Jabal Druze</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jabal al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> is a volcanic upland in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suwayda">Suwayda</a>, settled mainly by Druze from the 18th century. Under the French mandate it formed a separate Druze state from 1921. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-syrian-revolt">Great Syrian Revolt</a> broke out there in 1925 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-al-atrash">Sultan al-Atrash</a>, and the region joined the Syrian Republic in 1936.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jabal-al-druze.jpg" alt="Jabal al-Druze" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KFZI310 at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tell_Qeni.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Druze moved east to the Hauran in waves from 1685, and after the battle of Ain Dara in 1711 many Yamani Druze followed, laying the foundation of the <b>Jabal al-Druze</b>, while Maronites became the dominant group in Mount Lebanon.</blockquote><a href="/religion/druze-faith">Druze faith · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1921 France reached an agreement with Druze leaders granting the mountainous <b>Jabal al-Druze</b>, south of Damascus, autonomy under French protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/jabal-druze-state">Jabal Druze State</a></li><li><blockquote>France merged the State of Damascus and the State of Aleppo into the State of Syria with effect from 1 January 1925, with Damascus as capital and Subhi Barakat as president; the Alawite and <b>Jabal Druze</b> states remained separate.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-syria">State of Syria</a></li><li><blockquote>The federation had a single budget, customs and currency, but nationalists criticised it as a French device that kept Lebanon, the <b>Jabal Druze</b> and Alexandretta apart from Syria.</blockquote><a href="/state/syrian-federation">Syrian Federation</a></li></ul></section>
